    "purpose": "The session handover step (R-OS-3): when a session generation closes and the collector hands the day out as one write batch, this hive turns that batch into ONE fresh, recency-weighted summary - more from the last turns, phase-out before, hard facts kept - and emits it as a system.handover update, the form an llm cell consumes without a provider call. The next generation opens lazily and wakes up with yesterday instead of with nothing, so the transition is not noticeable. A glue cell shapes the prompt (the prompt lives in the glue phase, not in the llm config), a writer llm writes the prose, and a failed call leaves on its own error route instead of being swallowed. Since 2.1.0 the four weighting knobs of `./prep` are params of that cell instead of environment substitution tokens ([#138](https://github.com/mmeyerlein/meclaw/issues/138)): an `override_params` entry can name them, so two summarizers in one colony weight their days apart.",
    "use_when": "One per agent, behind the collector's 'write' route. Instantiate with ctx.model (a resolved literal, K-H2) and wire the ports in the SAME mutation: the batch lane in, the summary route to wherever the handover belongs - typically the agent llm's system.handover slot - and a drain or alarm for summary_error. This hive is the ONLY writer of system.handover (R-OS-1 slot discipline).",
    "not_in_scope": "No session lifecycle: the keeper decides when a generation ends and the collector produces the batch - this hive only consumes it. No memory extraction: one summary is written, what else the batch is worth belongs to the memory hive the same batch can also be routed to. No retry: retry is edge business; the template emits summary_error once and the parent tree decides. No tool use in the writer, and no judgement about where the summary lands - the parent edges decide the target.",
    "examples": [
      "PORTS - Entry (onto the hive path, the parent edge names the lane): in_batch, wired as {from: <collector>, to: <summarizer>, condition: has(hop.route) && hop.route == 'write', modifier: {set_hop: {route: 'in_batch'}}} - it consumes exactly the collector's close-batch form (messages[] the whole day, top-level slot 'rounds', hop.session_id/turn_count/round_count). Exit (from the hive path, on hop.route): 'summary' -> one system.handover update per batch (hop.session_id, hop.summary_chars), typically to the agent llm; 'summary_error' -> the degradation lane (hop.error_code), MUST be drained by the parent.",
      "a 40-turn day closes -> the newest 12 turns travel verbatim, the older 28 condensed to 200 chars each and counted, tool rounds as capped previews -> one summary on route 'summary'",
      "the provider fails with a 500 -> exactly one message on route 'summary_error' with error_code provider_error, and no half-summary behind it",
      "an empty session closes -> the prompt says '0 turns' and the shipped instructions demand a short honest summary instead of invention"
    ]
  },
